      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so it's basically just about finding those middle-man columns that don't belong to the main key. i've been trying to do this for a few hours and it feels like i'm just chasing my tail trying to find every single one of these dependencies. what's a good rule of thumb or a mental checklist i can use so i don't miss any of these hidden dependencies when i'm auditing a large table?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A helpful mental checklist is simple: take any non-key column and ask, \"Does this column determine the value of another non-key column?\" If the answer is yes, you likely have a transitive dependency that needs fixing. Another good rule is to look for attributes that describe *another* entity within the table; if so, that suggests moving the description to its own table. Regularly checking dependencies across all non-key attributes is the safest way to audit diligently.",
      "source": "target",
